1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,885
Every retailer wants service that is fast, accurate and personal, without putting money or trust at risk.

2
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:14,855
This is Everly, the AI companion ACME Retail gives each of its customers, built on Cognizant's Neuro SAN framework.

3
00:00:15,605 --> 00:00:25,340
We start on ACME's website sign-in, simulated for this demo. Every customer you will see is synthetic test data, stored in a live Databricks workspace.

4
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:32,380
The agents receive only what the website sends for a signed-in visitor, never a customer number typed into the chat.

5
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,115
Behind the scenes is a team of ten agents.

6
00:00:36,565 --> 00:00:43,555
Orbit, the orchestrator, is the only one that talks to the customer. Anchor handles intake and identity.

7
00:00:44,005 --> 00:00:51,365
Swift covers orders and delivery, Echo returns and refunds, Vault payments and billing, and Halo loyalty.

8
00:00:51,815 --> 00:01:03,255
Pulse is the product advisor, Aura the personal stylist, Sentinel checks policy and risk, and Guardian follows up after the chat ends. To the customer, it is all simply Everly.

9
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:09,180
What makes this practical to build is Neuro SAN. This is the real network file.

10
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:19,635
Each agent is a block of configuration: a description, its instructions, and the tools it may call. Each tool is a description that points to a Python class.

11
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:29,325
Orbit simply lists the specialists as its tools. Neuro SAN does the orchestration: it routes each request between the agents and returns the results.

12
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:38,570
It also carries private session data, such as the verified customer number, beside the conversation, where the model can neither read it nor change it.

13
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:53,410
The business rules live in ordinary code. This is the policy function: requests from an assistant are read-only, and refunds above a hundred and fifty dollars need a person. Because it is code, it is unit tested, not buried in a prompt.

14
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:59,010
Adding a specialist means adding one more block of configuration, and its tools.

15
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:03,090
First: two questions, one answer.

16
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:12,385
Daniel has signed in on the ACME website. He asks where the money is for the running shoes he returned, and how many loyalty points he has.

17
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:17,875
The model's thinking time is sped up here, so we can watch the agents work.

18
00:02:18,325 --> 00:02:29,820
Orbit asks Anchor to open a case. Anchor confirms Daniel's identity from his signed website session, in code, with no security questions, and splits the message into two issues.

19
00:02:30,270 --> 00:02:40,965
Echo, the returns specialist, calls its tools. Each one runs a Unity Catalog function on ACME's Databricks SQL warehouse, filtered to the signed-in customer.

20
00:02:41,415 --> 00:02:50,870
The records show his return was scanned at the warehouse nine days ago, but no refund was ever started. Meanwhile, Halo reads his loyalty profile.

21
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:57,225
Echo proposes a refund, and Sentinel checks it against ACME's policy and Daniel's account.

22
00:02:57,975 --> 00:03:08,955
A hundred and nineteen dollars is within the automatic limit, so it is allowed. The action layer carries out the refund, and reads it back to confirm it, rather than taking the model's word.

23
00:03:09,405 --> 00:03:14,885
Daniel gets one clear answer to both questions, and was never asked for an order number.

24
00:03:15,635 --> 00:03:25,885
The case record shows both issues, the policy decision, and the executed refund with its reference. Every step is stored as data, so it can be audited later.

25
00:03:26,635 --> 00:03:29,420
And here is what that answer was grounded in.

26
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:38,270
On the left is the Unity Catalog function the tools call. The customer number is supplied by the system, never taken from the conversation.

27
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:51,795
In the middle are the actual records Databricks returned for Daniel, kept as evidence on his case: the shoes, the warehouse scan on the seventh of October, no refund record, and the eligibility check that made a refund applicable.

28
00:03:52,245 --> 00:04:08,460
On the right is the resulting case record. To be clear about what is live: the Databricks workspace, warehouse and queries are real; the customers and orders are synthetic test data. Everly only reads from Databricks; its actions are recorded in its own action store.

29
00:04:10,010 --> 00:04:17,860
Second: people in the loop, and a follow-up that doesn't forget. Priya Raman, a platinum customer, signs in.

30
00:04:18,610 --> 00:04:23,240
She asks about the refund on her two hundred and eighty nine dollar wool overcoat.

31
00:04:23,990 --> 00:04:33,745
Echo reads her records. The refund was started on the sixth of October, but it has been stuck in processing ever since, so Echo proposes to retrigger it.

32
00:04:34,195 --> 00:04:41,680
That is above the hundred and fifty dollar automatic limit, so Sentinel's policy check returns: a person must approve it.

33
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:45,350
Everly doesn't guess, or over-promise.

34
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:55,225
It tells Priya her refund has been sent to a colleague for review, gives her the reference, and makes no promise about when, or whether, it will be approved.

35
00:04:55,975 --> 00:05:01,970
The case shows the decision: approval required. The request now waits in the review queue.

36
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:10,720
This is where Guardian comes in. A typical chatbot forgets an issue once the chat closes. Guardian keeps following it.

37
00:05:11,470 --> 00:05:17,165
In the Inbox is the follow-up Guardian has opened. Let's simulate what happens next.

38
00:05:17,915 --> 00:05:26,185
A colleague approves the refund. The action is carried out, and Priya is told it has been approved and started, with the same reference.

39
00:05:26,935 --> 00:05:37,725
Then the refund completes. Guardian checks the records again, sends Priya a short update, and closes the issue, but only once a second read of the data confirms the outcome.

40
00:05:38,175 --> 00:05:44,225
Its updates use fixed wording filled from the records, and respect quiet hours and message limits.

41
00:05:45,775 --> 00:05:57,130
Third: Aura, the personal stylist. Everly isn't only there when something goes wrong. Customers who opt in to Personal Stylist Mode have Aura. We sign in as Angelica.

42
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:03,510
In the Launches tab, ACME's merchandiser launches a new product: a cashmere scarf.

43
00:06:04,260 --> 00:06:10,390
Aura scores the launch against the wardrobe, sizes and preferences of every customer who opted in.

44
00:06:10,840 --> 00:06:19,180
The scoring is code, not the model. Only a score of eighty or more, with something the customer already owns to name, earns an invite.

45
00:06:19,930 --> 00:06:27,390
Of the fifteen customers who opted in, four are invited, nine scored below eighty, and two had turned this category down.

46
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:41,675
Angelica owns a navy wool overcoat, so the scarf is a strong match. Her Stylist tab shows the pick and every reason for it: the overcoat, her style, her colours, her usual price range, and the season.

47
00:06:42,425 --> 00:06:45,645
She asks Everly about the scarf she was sent.

48
00:06:46,395 --> 00:06:56,335
Orbit brings in Aura, who reads the invite that was actually sent, so the answer can only describe that product, its real price, and the reasons that were recorded.

49
00:06:57,085 --> 00:07:05,855
Aura explains it: what it is, what it costs, and why it suits her, without pushing her to buy, or claiming anything is reserved for her.

50
00:07:06,605 --> 00:07:09,290
And when she replies that it's not for her,

51
00:07:10,040 --> 00:07:18,275
Aura records that feedback in code, so similar pieces will be picked less often, and Angelica can switch Stylist Mode off at any time.

52
00:07:19,025 --> 00:07:24,615
Invites share the same message limits as service updates, and service always comes first.

53
00:07:26,165 --> 00:07:34,570
Fourth: Pulse, the product advisor, who suggests products from what a customer actually bought. Isabella Nguyen signs in.

54
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:37,970
She asks what goes with her chinos.

55
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:49,760
Pulse calls ACME's recommendation engine. The suggestions are chosen by code, from her real orders and the catalog. The model only explains them, and never invents a product.

56
00:07:50,510 --> 00:08:02,730
She returned her chinos because they were the wrong size, so the first suggestion is the same chinos in another size, then a belt and an oxford shirt that go with them. Every card comes from ACME's catalog data.

57
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:12,685
Fifth: an external AI assistant asks Everly. More and more customers have their own AI assistants, and Everly is ready for them.

58
00:08:13,435 --> 00:08:23,515
On the left is Benjamin's assistant, here a scripted MCP client. It connects to Everly's assistant gateway with an access token Benjamin granted it, read-only.

59
00:08:23,965 --> 00:08:31,775
Its first call, get order status, answers instantly from the records: his shoes are with FedEx, still in transit.

60
00:08:32,525 --> 00:08:46,550
Then the assistant calls ask support, passing only Benjamin's question. There is no customer field. The gateway validates the token, looks up its grant, and gives Neuro SAN Benjamin's customer number in the private session data.

61
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:57,625
The request goes through Orbit and Anchor to Swift, the orders and delivery specialist, who finds the parcel hasn't moved from Memphis in six days, and proposes a carrier trace.

62
00:08:58,075 --> 00:09:03,680
But Sentinel's policy check sees the assistant channel, which is read-only, and denies it.

63
00:09:04,430 --> 00:09:12,425
The assistant polls get answer, and receives a structured result: the request number, its status, the reply and the case.

64
00:09:12,875 --> 00:09:19,220
Everly explains the stall, and tells Benjamin he can open the trace himself in ACME's own app or website.

65
00:09:19,970 --> 00:09:29,415
Back in the console, the assistant's request appears on the map as its own lane: Orbit, Anchor, Swift and Sentinel, answered, and marked read-only.

66
00:09:29,865 --> 00:09:39,530
Even if the model had submitted the trace anyway, the action layer re-checks the same policy before anything runs, and refuses it. Nothing was executed.

67
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:42,645
Here is the evidence from that run.

68
00:09:43,095 --> 00:09:50,615
The gateway's tools take no customer number at all. Identity comes only from the validated access token and its grant.

69
00:09:51,065 --> 00:10:02,925
The trace shows each step inside Everly, from identity, through Swift's findings, to Sentinel's denial and the action layer's refusal. The decision log records the reason: confirm in channel.

70
00:10:03,375 --> 00:10:10,730
And the assistant receives its answer as structured data, with the case reference, so it can follow up on the same case later.

71
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:23,315
What we have demonstrated is more than a chatbot. Everly brings together specialist agents, customer data, business rules and external integrations, through Cognizant's Neuro SAN framework.

72
00:10:23,765 --> 00:10:34,180
This modular approach helps developers build and extend agentic applications, while keeping business decisions in testable code, and sensitive actions under controlled execution.

73
00:10:34,930 --> 00:10:43,485
Customers get a more connected service experience, while the retailer retains oversight, traceability and control. Thank you.

